{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1455124-vtm-revised-1998-is-aggravate-damage-even-needed","id":1455124,"title":"VTM Revised (1998) - Is aggravate damage even needed?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-07-18T13:36:24-07:00","id":1455124,"title":"VTM Revised (1998) - Is aggravate damage even needed?","content":"Looking at 1998 Revised rules for VTM (still the most popular version in my country, probaby because noone wants to buy new books every few years) I wonder if you even need agg damage to deal with other vampires cosidering Potence is no Blood cost auto success per dot and Celerity is 1 BP for as many extra actions as you you have dots in celerity. So Brujah with 2 Potence and 2 Celerity on start with Sword STR+3 could own almost anything. Also since Initiative is DEX+WITS, you have those high to start first (and have high dodges and high parries) and you could deal like what: 7 average dmg per attack with sword? 3 action that's 21 lethal damage to soak.<br /> <br /> But on the other hand you had OP Hold+Bite with auto-success Potence but then you are sitting ducks.<br /> <br /> I don't recommend 1v1 a warewolf but with Resources 5 you can that sword silvered no issue for 21 unsoakable dmg if you win Initiative (otherwise you die so it's last fight for one of you). With Clerity 5 and Potence 5 that would be...ugh.<br /> <br /> Would probably add Auspex to counter Obfuscate.<br /> <br /> Crazy when you compare it to V5 now.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24341,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24341-viben","user":"Viben","date":". 07-18-2021, 04:41 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24341-viben","name":"Viben","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"72"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-18T17:03:21-07:00","id":1455140,"title":"","content":"Ultimately? No. You're correct that Agg is not the king of combat. Having Agg is better than not everything else being equal, but you don't just pick an Agg attack and rule the fight.<br /> <br /> The big advantages for Agg are:<br /> <br /> 1) Anti-healing. A soak monster to counter your damage monster is at a disadvantage (that's not a bad thing, you don't want combat to stall completely), but if they're not spending blood while you are, they can use it to heal bashing/lethal damage. Once Agg comes into play combat-healing is off the table (even for lupines combat healing is harder to do once Agg is being tossed around). If you ambush someone with non-Agg and they get away but took some damage, they can probably feed and heal up completely before you get to them again. If you do the same with Agg, you have a lot more time to track them down and finish them while they're still weakened.<br /> <br /> 2) Concealed. Most Agg sources for vampires are things they always have on them. It might get obvious once it's clear violence is going to break out, but you can walk down the street as a vampire with your fangs hidden, powers like Protean and Serpentis not having any outward signs, and so on. The Potence route means relying on hand-to-hand weapons. In most places where you're going to find a significant number of vampires, walking around with a sword isn't going to go well. At best it's going to draw attention and bar you from specific locations, at worst you're going to be dealing with the police instead of whatever vampire business you had to deal with. If you want to not be obvious, you limit your average damage by using low damage-tag weapons (and good concealable armors are easier to get these days).<br /> <br /> So agg isn't needed, but it has its uses. Plus, if we're looking at the wider WoD, there are things that can get good lethal soak pools, but have shit Agg soak. So what you're dealing with can vastly shift the value of lethal vs. agg.<br /> <br /> You might want to check out V20 though, there was a 13 year gap there....and it rebalanced Potence and Celerity a bit. The vast majority of V20 is backwards compatible with Revised, so it's pretty easy to use your Revised collection with the V20 rules.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-18T17:20:31-07:00","id":1455146,"title":"","content":"It's to emphasize how you can't bounce back from getting charred by the sun. You will remain crispy critter made up of well-done and outright burnt meat until you drain a lot of humans (roughly as many as typical slices in a loaf of bread, most likely). You also get to roll Stamina + Fortitude against lethal, and unless you're beheaded or your heart is destroyed, it might not result in final death like agg does.<br /> <br /> As far as W:tA goes, you're better off not trying to make the comparison. It's a different genre where they're not punished nearly as much for butting heads, though there's always a background risk of dying in a horribly grotesque fashion that would make V:tM very discouraging if it had to play by the same flow of action, since the sell for Vampire is being immortal as long as you avoid fighting. Which is why Kindred in Revised, IIRC, had a one-turn warmup for Celerity actions, meaning that they took effect on the turn after the blood was spent, and why Garou and Fera have a Gift that flat out adds ten to their initiative calculation, plus ten more for every point of Gnosis they bother to spend to crank it higher, and it isn't even the most dramatic influence on combat. Or, in other words, you will not win initiative, you will not conceal your presence (Sense Silver is another Gift that the same character type will have), and you will not get the drop on them. You engaged in the most predictable move of all time, and will summarily end up discovering what the Splatterpunk genre is and how it differs from Personal Horror.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-18T17:37:59-07:00","id":1455148,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1455124-vtm-revised-1998-is-aggravate-damage-even-needed?p=1455140#post1455140\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ultimately? No. You're correct that Agg is not the king of combat. Having Agg is better than not everything else being equal, but you don't just pick an Agg attack and rule the fight.<br /> <br /> The big advantages for Agg are:<br /> <br /> 1) Anti-healing. A soak monster to counter your damage monster is at a disadvantage (that's not a bad thing, you don't want combat to stall completely), but if they're not spending blood while you are, they can use it to heal bashing/lethal damage. Once Agg comes into play combat-healing is off the table (even for lupines combat healing is harder to do once Agg is being tossed around). If you ambush someone with non-Agg and they get away but took some damage, they can probably feed and heal up completely before you get to them again. If you do the same with Agg, you have a lot more time to track them down and finish them while they're still weakened.<br /> <br /> 2) Concealed. Most Agg sources for vampires are things they always have on them. It might get obvious once it's clear violence is going to break out, but you can walk down the street as a vampire with your fangs hidden, powers like Protean and Serpentis not having any outward signs, and so on. The Potence route means relying on hand-to-hand weapons. In most places where you're going to find a significant number of vampires, walking around with a sword isn't going to go well. At best it's going to draw attention and bar you from specific locations, at worst you're going to be dealing with the police instead of whatever vampire business you had to deal with. If you want to not be obvious, you limit your average damage by using low damage-tag weapons (and good concealable armors are easier to get these days).<br /> <br /> So agg isn't needed, but it has its uses. Plus, if we're looking at the wider WoD, there are things that can get good lethal soak pools, but have shit Agg soak. So what you're dealing with can vastly shift the value of lethal vs. agg.<br /> <br /> You might want to check out V20 though, there was a 13 year gap there....and it rebalanced Potence and Celerity a bit. The vast majority of V20 is backwards compatible with Revised, so it's pretty easy to use your Revised collection with the V20 rules.</div> </div> </div> </div>1) Yes but they can only heal X per turn. 7th gen vampire can only heal 3. With Celerity 4-5 you attack them 5-6 times, they can only heal 3 points after that. It gives them chance, but overall if they can't soak the rest - torpor.<br /> <br /> 2)About Concealed a sword - it's not that big of an issue in modern setting. If you are wealthy (Resources 4-5) you can easy get hand-crafted fencing sword hidden in crafty walking crane (which you can also use as Club against mortals) and let it pass as just a fashionable crane. That's just one example. You can also walk with guitar suitcase but have a swords in there (Desperados vibes? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />), inside hidden false bottom or something. Trench coats or long fashionable coats/furs also help to conceal blades if you are wealthy. High Charisma + Appearance character won't have much issue with passing if weapon is well concealed. Presence helps a lot too.<br /> <br /> But yeah, of course agg is sometimes needed, I agree with that, but for mortals and vampires a Revised Celerity melee character seems like he gets the job done even vs Fortitude vampires (that's probably becasue auto-success beats chance for success every time. 10 soak diff 6 won't beat auto min 8 dmg with Celerity). Also becasue dice splitting pool rule in Revised high Dex + Dodge + Celerity actually nets better defense than Stamina or Fortitude becasue you can do full defense action before Celerity gives you full offence vs opponents who have to split it.<br /> <br /> It's not like I don't like Revised but I think that maybe the whole problem with Celerity wasn't really Celerity but agg damage. Wish they never introduced any Agg dmg sources apart from Fire, Faith and Sunlight. All claws, fangs etc. should have been lethal from start.<br /> <br /> Because otherwise even STR 1 character with Brawl 3 but with Potence 5 will pretty much Hold + Bite into dust anyone without high Potence. And even then he can just blood buff Strength if needed. But if that combo dealt Lethal damage instead it would be still strong (I am fine with that) but not auto-kill.<br /> <br /> For me it's seems it's agg damage that made everything so though in Revised. Not saying Celerity in Revised isn't busted but combined with agg it's just.. pff.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24341,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24341-viben","user":"Viben","date":". 07-18-2021, 08:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24341-viben","name":"Viben","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"72"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-18T21:40:19-07:00","id":1455166,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Viben</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1455124-vtm-revised-1998-is-aggravate-damage-even-needed?p=1455148#post1455148\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1) Yes but they can only heal X per turn. 7th gen vampire can only heal 3. With Celerity 4-5 you attack them 5-6 times, they can only heal 3 points after that. It gives them chance, but overall if they can't soak the rest - torpor.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, a character with 5,000 XP invested in offense will demolish a character with 0 XP in defense. Constantly amping up the numbers doesn't really help illustrate anything because you can do that to either side.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 2)About Concealed a sword - it's not that big of an issue in modern setting. </div> </div> </div>They're illegal in most developed countries. If your vampire's enemies know you carry around one, they can just have variously minor pawns call in reports of a person threatening people with a sword cane, and then you have to deal with cops that need to search you, and then depending on where you are, could confiscate the weapon or even arrest you; forcing you to roll up those social Disciplines all the time instead of getting any of your goals done. In many jurisdictions sword canes are actually a worse idea to get caught with because they come with a worse penalty as &quot;disguised&quot; weapons (that is, weapons made to look like something that isn't a weapon) instead of simply a concealed weapon.<br /> <br /> Hiding them under clothing isn't automatic, and doesn't help against things like metal detectors or a simple pat down. Spotting concealed blades s a lot easier than people seem to think because blade long enough to be serious combat weapons don't flex or bend like a body does, and lots of really innocent motions or gestures completely give them away even if they look completely hidden when you're standing still.<br /> <br /> And of course, there's fashion and weather to consider. Trenchcoats don't really do you any good when it's 85 F outside, muggy, and walking around in a loose fitting trench just makes you even more suspicious looking. Loose fitting clothing goes in and out of fashion. When tight fitting is in, concealing weapons gets significantly harder even if you're wearing something called a trench coat.<br /> <br /> This is actually significantly worse in modern nights because well off individuals can afford to, and are expect to, have lots of different outfits for different occasions. If you want to avoid attention, it's harder to keep to clothing that gives you tactical advantages while remaining &quot;normal&quot; in various social contexts.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> High Charisma + Appearance character won't have much issue with passing if weapon is well concealed. Presence helps a lot too. </div> </div> </div>I'm thinking you need to be starting with other traits to get to that &quot;well concealed&quot; part before you get to the &quot;using supernatural powers to talk you way out of getting arrested,&quot; part.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But if that combo dealt Lethal damage instead it would be still strong (I am fine with that) but not auto-kill. </div> </div> </div>The Potence grappler combo is broken lethal or agg really.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-19T06:22:24-07:00","id":1455189,"title":"","content":"Eh, forget the sword.<br /> <br /> A knife (STR+1) will net you on average just one success less than the STR+3 sword and be eminently more concealable and less prone to raising questions even if it is found particularly if it\u2019s a utility knife (a folder that also has the the strap cutter and glass breaker tools for escaping a crashed car for example) and not a combat one.<br /> <br /> Regardless, when running my own games I drop the agg descriptor from everything that isn\u2019t a specific bane; fire/sun/true faith for vamps, silver for wolves, pattern rip magic for mages; everything else, including claws and fangs and the like is just lethal (including vampire fangs, protean claws and werewolf/mage attacks that aren\u2019t fire/sun/true faith for vampires).<br /> <br /> It makes Fortitude a bit more useful and the actual vampiric banes rightly the most terrifying things for a vampire to be exposed to.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-19T07:38:29-07:00","id":1455194,"title":"","content":"So something that happened in my games, is that I changed how soaking aggravated damage works. Kindred can soak aggravated with Stamina, and Fortitude, instead of granting soak dice, grants additional Health Levels and lowers soak difficulties. So aggravated damage remains difficult to heal, but isn't as ridiculous in combat. At first everything was fine, but once we got into some combats, the Gangrel player started having difficulty. It turned out that her Str +1 claws weren't cutting it at all, and she had difficulty inflicting damage (she only has Str 3). In V20 and earlier versions of VtM, 4 dice of aggravated damage can still be dangerous (especially post-Revised, as she has a high Dexterity). Now? Not so much. Sure, my rules prevent the uber-strong Potence using Gangrel from dominating combat, but it makes claws a lot less useful for less optimized Gangrel. I haven't decided what I want to do about this, but I just wanted to point out that aggravated damage is just fine as long as people aren't throwing around massive amounts of it- even Lure of Flames only does 3 damage a turn (albeit at diff 9 to soak; same as intense sunlight).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24241-lynceus","name":"Lynceus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24241_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-19T11:54:01-07:00","id":1455206,"title":"","content":"I do the Stamina soaks non-fire/faith/sunlight aggravated rule, so the only difference is healing times. Vanilla rules wise, if i recall correctly, You need aggravated weapons to harm not-quite corporeal enemies, like Vampires in Mist/Blood/Shadow forms. <br /> <br /> With healing times, that really comes into play if you're fighting strong gen vampires who can heal 2/3/4/whatever levels of lethal damage a turn. The higher level fires are also very important for weak vampires trying to deal with very tough old vampires. For low level play, it's not likely to make much of a difference; healing is there to alleviate wound penalties, the damage fixed is rarely enough to have you survive an extra round. <br /> <br /> I don't put a lot of stock in some versions of the melee weapon table. A Greatsword isn't +5, that's silly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-19T12:02:42-07:00","id":1455207,"title":"","content":"I miss DA 1e with Greatswords that did Str +6 and were diff 5 to attack...oh uh, but more to the point, healing in combat is HARD. There's a Stamina + Survival roll (diff 7) AFTER you spend your blood, and if you fail, it's wasted. If you botch, you actually take another level of damage! I'm always surprised when my players fail to invest in Survival, and find themselves unable to heal in combat. But really, the game is deliberately designed to make you want to avoid combat like the plague, so you'd think they'd learn...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24241-lynceus","name":"Lynceus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24241_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-19T12:06:17-07:00","id":1455209,"title":"","content":"Honestly I find WoD combat quite satisfying<br /> If you're in a small party<br /> and everyone knows what they're doing.<br /> ...houserules optional, but highly desirable.<br /> <br /> But most editions have melee tables designed by people who don't know how melee weapons work. Armour is similarly wonky, perhaps by design. Firearms are pretty ingenious below a certain power though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-19T12:23:27-07:00","id":1455210,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lynceus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1455124-vtm-revised-1998-is-aggravate-damage-even-needed?p=1455194#post1455194\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">... Kindred can soak aggravated with Stamina, and Fortitude, instead of granting soak dice, grants additional Health Levels and lowers soak difficulties.</div> </div> </div> </div>I like the sentiment here, but I think this is way over the top - it makes Fortitude the single top-tier, must-have discipline. Anyone with high Fortitude need not fear combat almost at all. No wonder why your player's Gangrel can't do damage.<br /> <br /> In KotE, Iron Mountain lowers soak difficulties - I always considered it superior to Fortitude only if the character's Stamina is 4+, and inferior to Fortitude otherwise. Basically, it's on-par with Fortitude, more or less. But health levels are a HUGE advantage - even the Huge Size merit, which costs 4 freebie points if I'm not mistaken, adds only one health level (and has the downside that your character is huge). This one health level adds significantly to survivability though, so adding up to 5 health levels with 5 Fortitude that has no downside? Kinda OP in my opinion.<br /> <br /> As for the Gangrel in general, I always felt the claws were way OP for a 2nd level discipline, and it's always been house-ruled in games I'm involved in that no discipline at less than 3 dots can inflict aggravated damage (Protean and Serpentis 2, for example, inflict lethal). They're still great disciplines even with this change, however.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26904,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26904-breggie","user":"breggie","date":". 07-19-2021, 03:26 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26904-breggie","name":"breggie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26904_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-19T14:45:18-07:00","id":1455216,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chris24601</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1455124-vtm-revised-1998-is-aggravate-damage-even-needed?p=1455189#post1455189\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A knife (STR+1)...</div> </div> </div> </div>As this is specifically a Revised thread, VtM Revised has knives go all the way down to Str-1 for small folding blades not intended for fighting (small pocket knives and straight razors are both marked as this in the books).<br /> <br /> As well, part of the reason for hand-to-hand weapons in this regard is higher damage tags. You can get Str+1 out of claws you can grow instantly after all.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Regardless, when running my own games I drop the agg descriptor from everything that isn\u2019t a specific bane; fire/sun/true faith for vamps, silver for wolves, pattern rip magic for mages; everything else, including claws and fangs and the like is just lethal (including vampire fangs, protean claws and werewolf/mage attacks that aren\u2019t fire/sun/true faith for vampires). </div> </div> </div>It's not a bad approach, but it's a lot easier on pure vampire than some of the other games.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-19T21:29:22-07:00","id":1455234,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>breggie</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1455124-vtm-revised-1998-is-aggravate-damage-even-needed?p=1455210#post1455210\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I like the sentiment here, but I think this is way over the top - it makes Fortitude the single top-tier, must-have discipline. Anyone with high Fortitude need not fear combat almost at all. No wonder why your player's Gangrel can't do damage.<br /> <br /> In KotE, Iron Mountain lowers soak difficulties - I always considered it superior to Fortitude only if the character's Stamina is 4+, and inferior to Fortitude otherwise. Basically, it's on-par with Fortitude, more or less. But health levels are a HUGE advantage - even the Huge Size merit, which costs 4 freebie points if I'm not mistaken, adds only one health level (and has the downside that your character is huge). This one health level adds significantly to survivability though, so adding up to 5 health levels with 5 Fortitude that has no downside? Kinda OP in my opinion.<br /> <br /> As for the Gangrel in general, I always felt the claws were way OP for a 2nd level discipline, and it's always been house-ruled in games I'm involved in that no discipline at less than 3 dots can inflict aggravated damage (Protean and Serpentis 2, for example, inflict lethal). They're still great disciplines even with this change, however.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Actually I made a similar change there as well, swapping Protean 2 and 3 (not unlike Requiem) and kicking Burning Blade up to a level 3 Ritual. Honestly, while Fortitude is better, it doesn't make you invincible. You still need Stamina to get actual soak dice (and maybe armor). I don't have any characters in my Chronicle who have Fortitude above 3 at this time, because even with those upgrades, Fortitude is a fairly weak Discipline, as all it does is protect you from damage; compared to powers that can help you bypass combat (or being hit) altogether, it's fairly &quot;meh&quot;. And the Gangrel player isn't up against heavy Fortitude users...yet. I was just commenting on the fact that the hard to soak nature of Feral Claws is a feature, not a bug. Unfortunately, Potence turns what is probably a fairly balanced ability and quickly warps the game around it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24241-lynceus","name":"Lynceus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24241_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-19T23:31:39-07:00","id":1455239,"title":"","content":"Personally, I allow all vampires to soak everything but Bane Aggravated (fire, sunlight, faith) with Stamina + Fortitude, and Fortitude also gives extra Health Levels*. It gives Fortitude a boost to bring it more in line with Potence.<br /> <br /> But, and this is a load-bearing but, I use the Streamlined Combat rules from V20DA. That means you don't roll damage or soak; they're just static values. As that winds up being net successes + Strength + weapon modifier + Potence damage, versus Stamina + Fortitude, you're more likely to do some serious damage. <br /> <br /> (These combat rules also negate the need to spend blood to get auto successes for Potence, since damage is no longer rolled, which is why Fortitude is now stronger. This may have been an oversight in V20DA, as they never reference this or how to handle it. Likewise, they don't mention how to deal with fire and sunlight, which will do no damage if static versus higher levels of Fortitude; I got around this by saying that all environmental damage requires you roll soak.)<br /> <br /> Using those fast combat rules, it's quite easy to kill a mortal in a single blow and vampires without Fortitude go down in a couple of turns if they don't heal. That seems about right me.<br /> <br /> *Each dot of Fortitude grants additional health levels as follows:<br /> <br /> \u2022 Mauled (-2), Crippled (-5)<br /> \u2022\u2022 Wounded (-2)<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022 Injured (-1)<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 Hurt (-1)<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 Bruised (OK), Incapacitated","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":5291,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","user":"adambeyoncelowe","date":". 07-21-2021, 02:20 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-20T12:12:49-07:00","id":1455273,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lynceus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1455124-vtm-revised-1998-is-aggravate-damage-even-needed?p=1455234#post1455234\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">... You still need Stamina to get actual soak dice (and maybe armor). I don't have any characters in my Chronicle who have Fortitude above 3 at this time, because even with those upgrades, Fortitude is a fairly weak Discipline, as all it does is protect you from damage; compared to powers that can help you bypass combat (or being hit) altogether, it's fairly &quot;meh&quot;. And the Gangrel player isn't up against heavy Fortitude users...yet. I was just commenting on the fact that the hard to soak nature of Feral Claws is a feature, not a bug. Unfortunately, Potence turns what is probably a fairly balanced ability and quickly warps the game around it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ok, good point. Just as with Iron Mountain in KotE, it makes soaking really reliable, but it doesn't provide any extra dice of it's own so by itself it's really nothing special. Combined with other things that do add soak dice, though, it can make for some pretty impressive soak results.<br /> <br /> Were I ever to run a VtM game, I think I would make aggravated damage be from fire/sunlight/faith only (as others on this thread have said), and also modify all 3 &quot;physical&quot; disciplines as follows:<br /> * Celerity would provide additional dice for dexterity dice pools, rather than extra actions. Extra actions could thus be achieved with the normal &quot;dice splitting&quot; rules since your dice pool is now much bigger. Costs 1 blood point to activate for a scene.<br /> * Potence would provide additional dice for strength dice pools, rather than auto-successes. Costs 1 BP to activate for a scene.<br /> * Fortitude would count as additional dots of stamina, rather than just soak dice. Also can roll Fortitude-only to soak aggravated (from fire/sunlight only). Does not cost BP and is always active.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26904,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26904-breggie","user":"breggie","date":". 07-20-2021, 03:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26904-breggie","name":"breggie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26904_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-20T16:13:30-07:00","id":1455282,"title":"","content":"I went halfway there. Odd dots of Potence boost your Strength trait, and even dots grant automatic successes to Str rolls and for the Willpower rolls used to perform a Feat of Strength. Odd dots of Celerity boost your Dexterity trait, and even dots grant a bonus movement or defense action (you can switch a Celerity action to offense for a bp). And, of course, odd dots of Fortitude grant bonus Bruised Health levels, and even dots reduce soak difficulties by 1.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24241-lynceus","name":"Lynceus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24241_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-21T06:02:30-07:00","id":1455302,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lynceus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1455124-vtm-revised-1998-is-aggravate-damage-even-needed?p=1455282#post1455282\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I went halfway there. Odd dots of Potence boost your Strength trait, and even dots grant automatic successes to Str rolls and for the Willpower rolls used to perform a Feat of Strength. Odd dots of Celerity boost your Dexterity trait, and even dots grant a bonus movement or defense action (you can switch a Celerity action to offense for a bp). And, of course, odd dots of Fortitude grant bonus Bruised Health levels, and even dots reduce soak difficulties by 1.</div> </div> </div> </div>That sounds pretty good actually - I had misunderstood and assumed that with 5 Fortitude, the character would have -5 soak diff and +5 bruised health levels, which is why I said I thought it was was overpowered.<br /> <br /> I like the system you use for the physical disciplines - I will steal it if I ever run a VtM game again, which I probably won't, but you never know.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26904-breggie","name":"breggie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26904_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}